  <h2>Do You Need a Visa?</h2>
  <p>Most visitors to mainland China need a tourist (L) visa before traveling. The standard tourist visa allows a stay of 30 days per entry. Here are the current (2026) costs by nationality:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>United States:</strong> $140 for a 10-year multiple-entry visa (the best deal if you plan to return)</li>
    <li><strong>United Kingdom:</strong> GBP 151 for a 2-year multiple-entry visa</li>
    <li><strong>Canada:</strong> CAD 100 for a single-entry visa</li>
    <li><strong>Australia:</strong> AUD 110 for a single-entry visa</li>
    <li><strong>EU countries:</strong> EUR 80-125 depending on nationality and entry type</li>
    <li><strong>Southeast Asian countries:</strong> Fees vary widely — check with your local Chinese embassy</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Processing time is typically 4-7 business days for standard service, or 2-3 days for express (additional fee). Apply at least 2-3 weeks before your trip to allow for any delays.</p>

  <h2>144-Hour Transit Without Visa (TWOV)</h2>
  <p>Travelers from 53 countries can enter China without a visa for up to 144 hours (6 days) if transiting through certain cities — <strong>and Guilin is one of them</strong>. This means if you fly into Guilin from one country and have a confirmed onward ticket to a third country, you can stay in the Guilin region for up to 6 days without any visa at all.</p>
  <p>Eligible countries include the US, UK, Canada, Australia, most EU countries, Japan, South Korea, and others. The key rule: your arrival must be from Country A, and your departure must be to Country B (not Country A). Hong Kong, Macau, and Taiwan count as separate "countries" for TWOV purposes — so flying Hong Kong -> Guilin -> USA works perfectly.</p>

  <h2>How to Apply (Standard Tourist Visa)</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Fill out the COVA form online</strong> at the Chinese Visa Application Service Center website for your country. The form is detailed — have your passport, flight bookings, and hotel reservations ready.</li>
    <li><strong>Book an appointment</strong> at your nearest Chinese embassy, consulate, or visa application center. In major cities (New York, London, Sydney, etc.), appointments can fill up 1-2 weeks in advance during peak travel seasons.</li>
    <li><strong>Prepare your documents:</strong> passport valid for at least 6 months beyond your planned stay with at least two blank visa pages, recent passport photo (33mm x 48mm, white background), printed COVA form, flight itinerary showing entry and exit from China, hotel bookings for every night of your stay, and the visa fee (payment methods vary by location).</li>
    <li><strong>Attend your appointment.</strong> You'll submit your documents and may be asked brief questions about your travel plans.</li>
    <li><strong>Collect your passport</strong> 4-7 business days later (or 2-3 days for express service).</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 p-6">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-stone-400">Common Questions</p>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-700">
      <li><strong>Can I apply by mail?</strong> Some visa centers offer mail-in service. Check your local center's website.</li>
      <li><strong>What if my itinerary changes after getting the visa?</strong> Not a problem. The visa allows entry at any port within its validity period.</li>
      <li><strong>Do children need their own visa?</strong> Yes, every traveler including infants needs their own visa.</li>
      <li><strong>Can I extend my visa while in China?</strong> Tourist visas can be extended once for up to 30 days at the local Public Security Bureau Exit-Entry Administration office.</li>
    </ul>
